  "final": {
    "evidence_sufficiency": "SUFFICIENT",
    "confidence": 88,
    "conclusion": "The available evidence supports local stability in semantic class under nominally identical repetition: none of 16 anchors showed semantic-class instability across five exact repeats. It does not support total response invariance, because broader observable response state can still vary. Small changes to prompt wording and prior conversational history meaningfully increase full-state distance relative to exact repetition, while leaving semantic class unchanged; the affected dimensions include claim state, action, evidence, uncertainty, assertion, referent, and abstention/request behavior. Prior history specifically produced claim flips in 9.4% and action flips in 18.8% of matched cases. Accordingly, explanations based on pervasive semantic randomness or frequent answer-class switching should be rejected for these tested conditions. Explanations based on sensitivity of secondary response features to wording and context should be retained. Strong claims about exact rates or mechanisms for claim state, abstention, referents, and evidence selection should remain cautious because measurement reliability was only moderate to poor for several of those dimensions, although semantic-class and action findings were measured reliably."
